# Accessibility and performance

WCAG 2.2 quick reference: https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/quickref/

## Accessibility baseline (WCAG 2.2 AA)

- Semantic HTML first: `header`, `nav` (with `aria-label` when there are several), `main` (provided by `BaseLayout`), `section` with a heading, `footer`, lists for lists, buttons for actions, links for navigation.
- Every image has `alt`; decorative images use `alt=""`. Icons inside buttons or links have a text label or `aria-label` on the control, and the SVG gets `aria-hidden="true"`.
- Colour contrast: 4.5:1 for text, 3:1 for large text and UI boundaries. daisyUI semantic pairs (`bg-primary` + `text-primary-content`) are designed for this; verify custom brand tokens with a contrast checker before adding them.
- Keyboard: everything operable with Tab, Enter, Space, Escape, and arrow keys where expected. Visible focus (`focus-visible:` ring) on every interactive element. Focus is never obscured by the sticky navbar (WCAG 2.4.11): `scroll-padding-top` in `global.css` stays at the navbar's taller height (`--navbar-bar`, 5rem), including after the bar shrinks on scroll. The pricing comparison sticks at `top-20`, that same 5rem: change them together.
- Target size: interactive targets at least 24x24 CSS pixels with spacing (WCAG 2.5.8); prefer 44px on touch-first controls.
- Forms: visible `<label>` for every field, errors announced in text next to the field and linked with `aria-describedby`, no reliance on colour alone, no asking for the same information twice in one flow (WCAG 3.3.7).
- Motion: honour `prefers-reduced-motion` (global CSS gives every animation and transition zero duration and delay); no auto-playing motion longer than five seconds without a pause control; no flashing content. Two exceptions on the home page, both the owner's decisions (2026-11-18). The industry strip scrolls continuously, pauses only while the pointer is over it, and has no pause button, so the keyboard cannot pause it; reduced motion shows its names in a static wrap with no animation. The light on the hero's benefit ring (`src/components/ui/ServiceOrbit.astro`) circles for as long as the page is open and nothing pauses it; reduced motion leaves it off. Do not extend these exceptions to other motion.
- Disclosure patterns: use native `<details>` or daisyUI `collapse` with proper `aria-expanded` when built from buttons. Modals and drawers trap and restore focus.
- Language: `BaseLayout` sets `lang="en"`. Mark content in other languages with `lang` on the element.
- The skip link to `#main-content` must stay first in the DOM.

## Performance budget

- Lighthouse budgets live in `lighthouse.budgets.json` (mobile performance 0.9+, desktop 0.95+, accessibility 1, best practices 0.95+, SEO 1, metric and transfer limits) and are enforced by `npm run perf:audit`. They are lab measurements; field Core Web Vitals come only from the live site.
- Ship JavaScript only for islands. A page with no interactivity should load zero component JS.
- Nothing but the page itself may block the first paint (2026-11-22). The stylesheet is inlined in the head (`astro.config.mjs`) and kept small (`.cursor/rules/styling-tailwind-daisyui.mdc`), and the SVG favicon is a data URL in `BaseLayout`, so no stylesheet or icon request stands between the page's arrival and its first paint. Do not add a `<link rel="stylesheet">`, a blocking `<script>` in the head, or an `@import` of a remote sheet; the fonts stay preloaded with `font-display: swap`. A script that reads layout (`offsetWidth`, `getBoundingClientRect`, `scrollWidth`) on load measures after the browser's own first layout (a ResizeObserver's first report, `requestAnimationFrame`) and never after its own DOM writes, as `src/lib/industry-strip.ts` does.
- Images: `astro:assets` with explicit dimensions, modern formats (Astro outputs WebP/AVIF), `loading="lazy"` below the fold, `fetchpriority="high"` on the hero image only.
- Fonts: Catamaran for text and Mukta for headings, self-hosted, latin subset, `font-display: swap`. At most these two families.
- Third-party scripts (analytics, chat, embeds) need an explicit decision, load after interaction or idle, and must not block rendering. Record them in `docs/architecture.md`. Two so far. Google Tag Manager's container script from `BaseLayout`'s head (the owner's decision, 2026-11-21): the `dataLayer` and `gtag()` lines of Google's snippet as given, and the script itself added by a small inline loader at the visitor's first pointer movement, press, key, touch, or scroll, or three seconds after `load`, whichever comes first (the owner's decision, 2026-11-22; until then it loaded `async` with the page, and PageSpeed Insights counted it and the tags it loads, about 500 KB, against the home page's LCP on mobile in some runs); about 116 KB from Google for the container script, within the third-party request budget of 6 in `lighthouse.budgets.json`. Version 2, published 2026-11-21, also loads the Google tag `G-L8TJ8LXX5Z` on the production hostname (`docs/architecture.md`). Version 3, the same day, adds the Meta pixel on the same trigger, three more requests on every live page that the audit, on localhost, never loads (`docs/performance.md`). Playwright answers the script with an empty one (`tests/e2e/fixtures.ts`). Tags are configured in the container, never added to the code: the Meta pixel's base code is a container tag, so no `fbq` stub or second copy of it goes in the page, which would send PageView twice. And Calendly's scheduler on the contact page (the owner's decision, 2026-11-20): fetched after `load` at idle, created only while its panel shows, with a link to the same event when it cannot load (`src/lib/booking-embed.ts`); its bytes and cookies are Calendly's, so `/contact/` has its own allowance in `lighthouse.budgets.json` (`docs/performance.md`).
- Avoid layout shift: reserve space for images, embeds, and client-only islands.

## Checks for this area

- Automated: `npm run test:e2e` runs `tests/e2e/a11y.spec.ts` (axe, WCAG 2.2 AA tags) and `tests/e2e/smoke.spec.ts` (skip link, landmarks) on every built page.
- Automated: `tests/e2e/motion.spec.ts` (reduced motion, five-second limit on motion that starts on its own), `tests/e2e/layout.spec.ts` (360 to 1920 px, screenshots), and `npm run perf:audit` (Lighthouse mobile and desktop with budgets, `docs/performance.md`).
- Manual for new templates: full keyboard pass (Alt+Tab to reach links in WebKit), VoiceOver spot check on macOS, zoom to 200%, reduced-motion mode, inspection of the layout screenshots. Automated scans cover a subset of WCAG 2.2; they are not a conformance claim.
